@startuml sequence_diagram_logout
autonumber
skinparam shadowing true
skinparam responseMessageBelowArrow true

title Sequence Diagram - AA02 Logout of an Externally Authenticated User (session mechanism owned by AA04)

actor User as "Authenticated User\n(signed in with Google)"
participant SPA as "SPA (Front-End)"
participant Routes as "AuthRoutes"
participant AuthFilter as "AuthMiddleware\n(verifyToken)"
participant Controller as "AuthController"
participant Session as "Session invalidation\n(AA04 - to be designed)"
participant Google as "Google Identity Services\n(OIDC Provider)"

User -> SPA : clicks "Log out"
activate SPA
SPA -> Routes : POST /api/v1/auth/logout\nAuthorization: Bearer <jwt>
activate Routes
Routes -> AuthFilter : verifyToken(req, res, next)
activate AuthFilter
AuthFilter --> Routes : next()  (req.user resolved)
deactivate AuthFilter
Routes -> Controller : logout(req, res)
activate Controller
Controller -> Session : end the current application session
activate Session
note right of Session
    Defined by AA04 (e.g. token revocation /
    server-side session removal), so the ended
    session cannot be reused. The flow is identical
    for local (AA01) and Google (AA02) sessions.
end note
Session --> Controller : ok
deactivate Session
Controller --> SPA : 204 No Content
deactivate Controller
deactivate Routes

SPA -> SPA : discard token and cached protected data;\nreset to Guest
SPA --> User : public page / login page
deactivate SPA

note over SPA, Google
    Google is NOT called: logout ends the LaPrizza application session only (AC 7).
    LaPrizza never stored Google access or refresh tokens, so there is nothing to revoke at Google.
    The user's Google session in the browser is not affected.
end note

@enduml